{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1779649367,"nonce":"KjbVlOPDJx6dWMaKJgx8IPIoV0M=","message":"Correction to the Current code evidence section above: shell interpolation stripped the inline code spans from the original issue body. The intended evidence is:\n\n- `createNetworkSubgraph` creates an internal node with id `input_mux`, so a child graph can legally have the same local node id as the parent.\n- `enterSubgraph` sets the active layer to `state.graph.subgraphs[nodeId]` or a freshly created subgraph, and pushes the parent layer to `graphStack`.\n- `deleteSelected` deletes selected node ids from the currently active `state.graph.nodes`. If the active graph is accidentally still the parent layer, or if React Flow/UI selection state is not isolated between same-named parent/child nodes, deleting `input_mux` inside the apparent child context can delete the parent node.\n- `restoreSnapshot` restores `graph`, `uiState`, `graphStack`, and `currentContext` independently, without validating that the active graph matches the breadcrumb/current subgraph layer.\n- `enterSubgraph` uses cached graphs and registry template graphs without an explicit defensive clone at the layer boundary.\n\nThe intended fix direction also includes validating/restitching restored snapshots so active `graph`/`uiState` and `graphStack` represent one coherent current layer, plus a regression test where parent and child both contain `input_mux`; deleting the child `input_mux` must preserve the parent node and parent task bindings.","files":null}]}